{number: 131}
{title: O Master, Let Me Walk with Thee}
{key: Eb}

{start_of_verse: 1}
O [Eb] Master, [Ab] let me [Eb] walk with Thee
In [Ab] lowly [Eb] paths of [Bb7] service [Eb] free;
Tell me Thy [Ab] secret; [Eb] help me bear
The [Ab] strain of [Eb] toil, the [Bb7] fret of [Eb] care.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 2}
Help [Eb] me the [Ab] slow of [Eb] heart to move
By [Ab] some clear, [Eb] winning [Bb7] word of [Eb] love;
They wayward [Ab] feet in [Eb] paths to guide
And [Ab] stay them [Eb] walking [Bb7] by Thy [Eb] side.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
Teach [Eb] me Thy [Ab] patience; [Eb] still with Thee
In [Ab] closer, [Eb] dearer [Bb7] compa-[Eb] ny,
In work that [Ab] keeps faith [Eb] sweet and strong,
In [Ab] trust that [Eb] triumphs [Bb7] over [Eb] wrong.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 4}
In [Eb] hope that [Ab] sends a [Eb] shining ray
Far [Ab] down the [Eb] future's [Bb7] broad'ning [Eb] way,
In peace that [Ab] only [Eb] Thou canst give,
With [Ab] Thee, O [Eb] Master, [Bb7] let me [Eb] live.
{end_of_verse}
